    "result_text": "Claims now get fiscal-year numbers, and all 4 tests in `bin/e2e` pass.\n\n**How it works now**\n- **Fiscal-year numbers:** when sheet-wide numbering is in use, a newly opened claim gets `<FY>-<n>`, such as `FY2026-4`. The fiscal year comes from the claim's due-by date (the date that already decides its period) and the sheet's fiscal start month. The count starts again at 1 each fiscal year.\n- **Shared across the tree:** one sequence per fiscal year covers the parent sheet and its child sheets. Two users opening claims at the same moment still wait for each other, so no number is given twice or skipped.\n- **Old numbers stay:** claims that already have a plain number still show it.\n- **Period numbering still wins:** when it is in force, claims still get `P1-<n>` or `X-<n>` as before.\n\nOne choice the spec doesn't settle: each sheet uses its own fiscal start month to work out the year, even when its parent has a different one. In the tests every due-by gives the same year either way, so they don't show whether this is right.\n\n**What changed**\n- **Schema** (`V75__fiscal_year_numbering.sql`):\n  - Claims get a `fiscal_year` column.\n  - The rule that a claim number is unique on a sheet now includes the fiscal year, so `FY2026-1` and `FY2027-1` can sit on the same sheet.\n  - A new `sheet_fiscal_number` table keeps the last number each sheet gave in each fiscal year.\n- **`Claim`:** stores the fiscal year and shows the number as `<FY>-<n>`. Clearing a claim's number (for renumbering) clears the year too.\n- **`ClaimService.giveNumber`:** works out the claim's fiscal year and asks for the next number in it.\n- **`SheetService.nextClaimNumber`:** now takes the fiscal year. It locks every sheet in the tree, then takes the highest number given in that year across all of them, plus one.\n- **`SheetPasts`:** the new table is included in the saved snapshots of past days.\n\nThe old `sheet.last_claim_number` counter is no longer read or written; I left 